5. Count weekdays (excluding weekends) from Tuesday, July 4 to Monday, October 23, 1995 (including both start and end). So range: Jul 4 to Oct 23. Compute days. Jul 4: Jul start 182, Jul 4 =182+3=185. Oct 23: Oct start 274, Oct 23=274+22=296. Inclusive days =296-185+1=112 days. Check: Jul 4-31=28 days (31-4+1=28), Aug 31, Sep 30, Oct 1-23=23, total=28+31+30+23=112, yes. Day-of-week: Jul 4 day185, (185-1)=184 mod7= 7*26=182, remainder2 => Tuesday? 184-182=2 so Tuesday (since 0 Sun,1 Mon,2 Tue). Start Tue. Oct 23 day296, (296-1)=295 mod7= 7*42=294, remainder1 => Monday. So start Tue, end Mon. Total 112 days. 112/7=16 weeks exactly? 7*16=112, so exactly 16 weeks. With start Tuesday, the 16 weeks will cover exactly 112 days from Tue to Mon? Since 7 days later is Monday? Actually day1 Tue, day7 Mon? Let's see: Tue (1), Wed (2), Thu (3), Fri (4), Sat (5), Sun (6), Mon (7). So day7 = Mon. So 16 weeks = 16 cycles of 7 days, ending on day112 which is Monday. So the range is exactly 16 full weeks (Tue to Mon). Each week has 5 weekdays (Tue,Wed,Thu,Fri,Mon? Wait Monday is weekday, but also Sat and Sun are weekend. In a week from Tue to Mon inclusive, the weekdays are Tue, Wed, Thu, Fri, Mon (5 days), and weekends are Sat and Sun. So yes, each full week has 5 weekdays. So total weekdays = 16 * 5 = 80. So Q5: 80.

6. How many Saturdays from Saturday, October 7, 1995 to Sunday, December 17, 1995 inclusive. Count Saturdays. Range: Oct 7 to Dec 17. Compute days. Oct 7: Oct start 274, Oct 7=274+6=280. Dec 17: Dec start 335, Dec 17=335+16=351. Inclusive days = 351-280+1=72 days. Day-of-week: Oct 7 day280, (280-1)=279 mod7= 7*39=273, remainder6 => Saturday? 279-273=6, so Saturday. Start Sat. Dec 17 day351, (351-1)=350 mod7=0 (since 7*50=350), so Sunday. So start Sat, end Sun. Total 72 days. Need number of Saturdays. Since start is Saturday, then Saturdays occur every 7 days. In a range of N days inclusive starting on Saturday, number of Saturdays = floor((N+6)/7)? Actually we can compute: First Sat is day1, then day8, etc. Number = 1 + floor((N-1)/7). Since N=72, (N-1)=71, floor(71/7)=10, so total = 1+10=11. Alternatively, 72 days = 10 weeks + 2 days, so there are 11 Saturdays if start is Saturday? Let's compute: 10 weeks give 10 Saturdays (since each week has one Saturday), plus the first day is Saturday, so that's one, plus the extra two days? Actually 10 weeks is 70 days, which would include the first Saturday? Let's see: If start Sat, then days: Sat (1), then after 7 days: next Sat (8), ... after 70 days (10*7) day70? Day1 Sat, day8 Sat, ... day64? Actually day1 + 7*(k-1). For k=10, day = 1+7*9=64? That's not 70. Let's compute: number of Saturdays in 70 days starting Sat: Since 70/7=10 exactly, there will be 10 Saturdays? Actually if you have 70 days from Sat to Fri? But 70 days inclusive from Sat: days 1 to 70, the Saturdays are at 1,8,15,...,64? Because 1+7*9=64, that's 10 Saturdays? 1,8,15,22,29,36,43,50,57,64 → indeed 10. Then extra days day71 Sat, day72 Sun gives one more Saturday (day71). So total 11. So Q6: 11.

